<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">René of Anjou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1409">1409</a>–<a href="/years/1480">1480</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Good King René</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">René of Anjou was Duke of Anjou, Count of Provence and Duke of Bar, and by marriage Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, as well as titular king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. He lost Naples to Alfonso of Aragon in 1442. Known as Good King René, he was a patron of art, letters and tournaments at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angers">Angers</a> and Aix. After his death Anjou and Provence passed to the French crown.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rene-of-anjou.jpg" alt="René of Anjou" width="320" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicolas Froment · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_Roi_Ren%C3%A9.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>René was born on 16 January 1409 in the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angers">Angers</a>, the second son of Duke Louis II of Anjou and Yolanda of Aragon. His sister Marie married the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vii-of-france">Charles VII of France</a>. In 1419, when he was ten, he was legally married to Isabella, elder daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-duke-of-lorraine">Charles II, Duke of Lorraine</a>, and he was brought up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>.</p><p>He joined the French army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> in 1429 and was present at the consecration of Charles VII. He inherited the duchy of Bar in 1430 and that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> in 1431, but the heir-male Antoine de Vaudemont, with Burgundian help, defeated him at Bulgneville in July 1431, and he was a prisoner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> until 1432. On the death of his brother Louis III in 1435 he became Duke of Anjou.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Joanna II, queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, offered René her kingdom, and he sailed for Naples in 1438. Alfonso V of Aragon also claimed it and laid a six-month siege to the city in 1441. René returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1442, keeping the title of king of Naples but never recovering effective rule. In 1444 he took part in the negotiations with the English at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>, and his younger daughter Margaret married Henry VI of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nancy">Nancy</a>. After his second marriage, to Jeanne de Laval, he took a less active part in public affairs and turned to poetry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">miniature painting</a>, gardening and raising animals.</p><p>He patronised the painter Barthélemy d'Eyck and wrote two allegorical works, Le Mortifiement de vaine plaisance and Le Livre du Cuer d'amours espris, and a tournament book describing the rules of a tournament. He founded the Ordre du Croissant, which did not survive him. His house declined in his old age. His son John died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 1470, and his daughter Margaret was defeated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a> in 1471 and ransomed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a> in 1476.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>René retired to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aix-en-provence">Aix-en-Provence</a> and made a will in 1474 leaving Bar to his grandson René II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> and Anjou and Provence to his nephew Charles, count of Le Maine. Louis XI seized Anjou and Bar, and the duchy of Anjou lapsed to the crown, while the annexation of Provence was postponed until the death of Charles of Le Maine. René died on 10 July 1480 at Aix and was buried in Angers Cathedral. Nineteenth-century historians gave him the epithet the Good.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “René of Anjou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Duke Raoul was killed fighting for France at the Battle of Crécy in 1346, and in 1431 <b>René of Anjou</b>, who claimed Lorraine through his wife Isabella, was defeated and captured at the Battle of Bulgnéville by his rival Antoine de Vaudémont with Burgundian help.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It was granted as an appanage to Charles of Anjou in 1246, became a duchy in 1360 for Louis I, and its last great duke, <b>René of Anjou</b>, died in 1480.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-anjou">County of Anjou</a></li><li><blockquote>That year Charles of Maine, heir of <b>René of Anjou</b>, bequeathed it to Louis XI, and Provence was united with the Kingdom of France, while keeping its own estates and parliament at Aix.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-provence">County of Provence</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Antoine of Vaudémont claimed the Duchy of Lorraine and defeated <b>René of Anjou</b> at Bulgnéville in 1431.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-vaudemont">County of Vaudémont</a></li><li><blockquote><b>René of Anjou</b> inherited Bar in 1430 from his great-uncle, Cardinal Louis of Bar, and through his wife Isabella gained Lorraine in 1431.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bar">Duchy of Bar</a></li><li><blockquote>Metz fought the dukes of Lorraine and withstood a siege by Charles VII and <b>René of Anjou</b> in 1444.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-metz">Free Imperial City of Metz</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angers">Angers</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aix-en-provence">Aix-en-Provence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.47,-0.55&amp;year=1455" aria-label="Angers on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of René of Anjou"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4968 6063h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4968 6063h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.47,-0.55&amp;year=1455">What was here in 1455? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
